.TH ASKTIME 1
.SH NAME
asktime \- ask for the time when the machine starts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B /etc/asktime
[\ \fB\-a\fR\ ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B asktime
is run by /etc/rc each time XENIX starts. It shows the time the clock
starts from and asks for the time:
.PP
.nf
	Current System Time is Mon Oct 13 17:00:00 PDT 1986, 5:00 PM
	Enter the time as 3:36 PM or 10/14 9:05 AM, or Return to keep it:
.fi
.PP
Return keeps the time as it is. Otherwise, give a time, a date, or both,
as
.BR date (1)
takes them: 5:36 PM, 10/14 9:05 AM, 1986-10-14 17:36, and so on; XENIX's
own forms, hhmm, ddhhmm, mmddhhmm and yymmddhhmm, work as well. Anything
else asks again. The time of day is shown, and the examples given, on
the 12- or 24-hour clock and with month or day first, as
/etc/default/date says.
.PP
An XT has no clock that runs while the machine is off. XENIX starts its
clock from the date in the root file system's super-block, the last time
it wrote there, so after the machine has been off, the clock is behind by
as long as it was off. With a real-time clock (/dev/clock),
.B asktime
first sets the clock from it, through /etc/setclock, and afterwards
sets it to the time given.
.PP
With
.BR \-a ,
which /etc/rc gives when the machine starts by itself, it waits 30
seconds for an answer, then keeps the time and goes on.
.PP
It also gives the kernel the machine's name from /etc/systemid, with
.BR hostname (1),
so that
.B uname \-n
prints it: the kernel forgets it at every start.
.PP
And it makes the console's cursor a block, with
.BR cursor (1),
for every account from then on; while the machine starts, before it,
the cursor is the usual line.
.SH FILES
.TP 20
/etc/default/date
CLOCK=12 or 24, DATE=mdy or dmy
.TP
/etc/systemid
the machine's name
.TP
/dev/clock, /etc/setclock
a real-time clock, if there is one
.SH SEE ALSO
cursor(1), date(1), hostname(1)
.SH HISTORY
An
.B asktime
command was part of XENIX's start-up. This one takes its place.
.SH AUTHORS
This asktime was written for the xenix86-devsys system tools.
.SH BUGS
XENIX's own asktime takes only [yymmdd]hhmm, and makes a year from 00 to
69 into 1970.
